WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.820
Protocol parsers need resource limits because input can be malformed, oversized, repeated, slow, or unexpectedly complex.

2
00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:17.000
A parser with no limits can spend too much memory, CPU, queue space, or session state on input that should have been rejected early.

3
00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:28.000
Useful limits include maximum message size, field count, nesting depth, queue depth, session count, output size, and processing time.

4
00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:34.020
These limits should be chosen for the protocol and deployment environment, then enforced consistently.

5
00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:39.560
Documentation helps operators understand what the parser will accept and what it will reject.

6
00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:43.200
Basic validation should happen before expensive work.

7
00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:55.140
Check that a message is plausible, complete, supported, and within size limits before allocating large buffers, performing expensive transformations, touching storage, or dispatching application actions.

8
00:00:55.420 --> 00:01:02.380
Do not allocate memory directly from untrusted length or count fields without checks and configured limits.

9
00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.880
A field may be syntactically valid but operationally too large.

10
00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:13.540
Allocation, indexing, copying, and loop bounds should all follow validated and bounded values.

11
00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:19.420
Timeouts, backpressure, and controlled disconnect behavior help preserve availability.

12
00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:22.720
A slow peer should not hold parser resources forever.

13
00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:25.540
A full queue should fail predictably.

14
00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:29.640
A session that violates the protocol should release resources.

15
00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:35.180
Availability is part of parser security, especially for long-running services and embedded systems.
